<h1>Well Drilling in Central Florida: New Wells Done Right</h1>
<p>Drilling a well in Central Florida means drilling into one of the most productive aquifer systems in the country — but the paperwork matters as much as the rig. New wells need permits, setbacks from septic and property lines, casing, grouting, and disinfection before the first glass of water.</p>
<p>Licensed Florida drillers handle that chain end to end. Whether it is a household well for a new build on a fringe lot, a dedicated irrigation well to get a big lot off city water bills, or a replacement well for a failing one, the quote should name depth, casing, pump, and tank — not just a per-foot number.</p>
<div class="card"><h2>What we handle</h2><ul><li>Household wells for new builds and off-grid lots</li>
<li>Dedicated irrigation wells</li>
<li>Replacement wells for failed casing or low yield</li>
<li>Permit, casing, and disinfection included by the driller</li></ul></div>
<h2>Common questions</h2>
<h3>How much does it cost to drill a well in Central Florida?</h3><p>Total cost depends on depth, casing, and the pump package. Get itemized quotes — drilling, casing, pump, tank, and permits as separate lines.</p>
<h3>Do I need a permit to drill a well in Florida?</h3><p>Yes. Water management district and county rules apply, and licensed drillers pull the permits as part of the job.</p>
